.DS_Store
.env
/node_modules/
*.tsbuildinfo

# React Router
/.react-router/
/build/

# Phage Hunters photo sources. Only the generated WebP is committed.
/public/phage-hunters/*.jpg

# The content build product. Git holds markdown only; D1 holds the only
# rendered copy, and this file exists on disk because build:content wrote it
# (check-all and ship build it before anything reads it). It was committed and
# byte-gated until the artifact arc; template-refs.json and assets.json stay
# committed because they are repo facts with no database owner.
/content/generated/posts.json

/content/generated/pages.json
/content/generated/dictionary.json
/content/generated/procedures.json
/content/generated/roster.json
/content/generated/phages.json
/content/generated/registry.json

# The CV's rows, which sync:content writes to D1 (docs/CV.md). Derived from content/cv/ and the publications by
# build:content, on the same footing as pages.json.
/content/generated/cv.json

# The colophon's stack data. Same class as posts.json, and it became one on
# 2026-09-08 (ruling 39a): it is derived entirely from tracked sources
# (package.json, wrangler.jsonc.example, drizzle/, .nvmrc, stack-notes.json),
# so committing it made every change to any of those a two-file change that
# only a machine running build:stack could complete. Renovate cannot run
# build:stack, so every dependency PR arrived red with no defect in it.
# build:stack runs before the gates in check-all, in ship's build step and in
# CI, exactly where build:content does.
/content/generated/stack.json

# The owner's identity, derived from the CV (app/lib/identity.mjs). build:content and the install step write
# it, so a CV change reaches the site through the next deploy and no copy of it is committed to go stale.
/app/lib/identity.generated.mjs

# The generated publication artifacts: the rows sync:content writes to D1 (twins and raw Crossref records
# included). Derived from content/publications/ by build:content, so
# committing them would make every corpus change a three-file change that only a machine could complete.
/content/generated/publications.json

# The paper twins were files here until publications moved to D1 (a Worker route serves them now). A checkout that
# built them before the move still has them, ignored, and the asset handler would serve them ahead of the route,
# so check:machine-readable refuses any that remain (publications-twins). Delete them; nothing writes them now.
/public/research/publications/*.md

# Claude Code. settings.json is committed; the local override never is.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Cloudflare
#
# THE SPLIT, written here so the next session does not undo it:
#   wrangler.jsonc          ignored. It differs from the tracked example in
#                           exactly two values, the account-scoped resource ids
#                           (D1 database_id, KV namespace id). Portfolio rule,
#                           capsid/conventions.md "Public-repo hygiene".
#   wrangler.jsonc.example  TRACKED, and it is the real description of this
#                           Worker's bindings: same bindings, same compat date
#                           and flags, same durable object migrations,
#                           placeholder ids. postinstall copies it into place on
#                           a fresh clone (scripts/bootstrap-config.mjs).
#   .dev.vars               ignored, always. Secrets are `wrangler secret`.
#
# The example never carries a real id. Adding a binding to wrangler.jsonc means
# adding it to the example in the same commit.
.mf
.wrangler
.dev.vars*
# Generated from wrangler.jsonc by `wrangler types`, on every postinstall and
# every typecheck. A derived file that regenerates whenever the config changes.
worker-configuration.d.ts
wrangler.jsonc
# Written by scripts/build-preview.mjs on every preview build: the preview database for migrations.
wrangler.preview-migrations.jsonc
# The watchdog Worker's config, on exactly the same split as the pair above.
#   wrangler.watchdog.jsonc          ignored. It differs from its example in one
#                                    value, ALERT_EMAIL, which is a personal
#                                    inbox rather than an account-scoped id. Not
#                                    a credential (it grants nothing, which is
#                                    why it is a var and not a wrangler secret),
#                                    and still kept out of git: this repo is
#                                    meant to be copied as a template, and a
#                                    personal address in a tracked config is
#                                    what leaks on the day somebody copies it.
#   wrangler.watchdog.jsonc.example  TRACKED, with the address on example.com,
#                                    which RFC 2606 reserves so it can never be
#                                    a real inbox.
# Keep this pair in step in both directions too, and never put the real
# address in the example.
wrangler.watchdog.jsonc

# The smoke credential for check:browser, read by scripts/lib/browser/credential.mjs via
# SMOKE_TOKEN_FILE. A bearer token, so it never lands in git, on the same
# footing as .dev.vars above. Minted with
# `node scripts/mint-smoke-token.mjs`; README.md carries the three places it
# then has to be set.
#
# IGNORED rather than merely untracked: ship refuses on any dirty tree, untracked
# files included.
.smoke-token

# Session scratch space. A working directory an investigation leaves behind,
# holding capture scripts and intermediate output that belong to nobody.
#
# IGNORED rather than merely untracked, for the reason /og-samples/ below is:
# `npm run ship` refuses on any dirty tree, untracked files included, so an
# unignored scratch directory blocks every ship window until somebody moves it
# out of the repo by hand. Added 2026-08-28, when it did.
/.scratch/

# Ship's own transcript, one timestamped file per run, written unconditionally
# by scripts/ship.mjs.
#
# IGNORED FOR TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS, and either alone would be enough.
#
#   1. The same one /.scratch/ above has: ship refuses on any dirty tree,
#      untracked files included. A log ship wrote itself, left untracked, would
#      make the next ship refuse because of the previous one. That is a gate
#      failing itself, which is worse than no log.
#   2. IT CARRIES ACCOUNT-SCOPED IDS. `wrangler deploy` prints its binding
#      table, which names the KV namespace id and CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID by
#      value, and the watchdog's table prints ALERT_EMAIL. Those are exactly the
#      values wrangler.jsonc and wrangler.watchdog.jsonc are gitignored to keep
#      out of this repository, so a tracked ship log would put them back.
#
# It carries no token. Checked before this was added rather than assumed:
# `wrangler secret` bindings do not appear in the deploy table at all, and the
# only OPERATOR_TOKEN value in ship.mjs goes into an Authorization header that
# is never echoed. What ship prints about the token is the FILE PATH, on one
# refusal branch, which is a path and not a credential.
/.ship-logs/

# check:browser's record of the long-running children it started, one JSON
# object per line, read and cleared by the NEXT run's preflight.
#
# IGNORED FOR THE `/.ship-logs/` REASON, which is the one that actually bites:
# ship refuses on any dirty tree, untracked files included, so a file the gate
# writes about itself would make the next ship refuse because of the last
# check:browser. It carries nothing else: process ids and the needles used to
# re-identify them, no paths outside this repo and no credential.
/.gate-pids/

# Social card review renders, written by `build:og -- --out og-samples`.
# Untracked because the PNG bytes are not deterministic and are deliberately
# kept out of the gated artifact, and IGNORED rather than merely untracked
# because `npm run ship` refuses on any dirty tree, untracked files included:
# without this line every ship window needs the directory moved out of the repo
# by hand, which is what happened on 2026-08-14.
/og-samples/

!.dev.vars.example
!.env.example

# The holdout suite is pulled here by improve-score.yml and must NEVER be committed:
# a hidden test set in the repo is one the attempt path can read, which defeats the
# measurement entirely.
.improve-holdout/

# Project MCP config: carries a driver bearer token
.mcp.json

# THE DESIGN-SYNC WORKING SET, for the /design-sync skill that exports this
# site's tokens and its four presentational components to claude.ai/design.
#
# What IS committed is the sync's INPUTS, under .design-sync/: config.json, the
# generator, the bundle entry, the authored previews, the conventions header and
# NOTES.md. Everything below is either a staged copy of the skill's own scripts,
# regenerated build output, or machine-local verification state, and a committed
# copy of any of them would be a second owner of something already owned
# elsewhere.
/.ds-sync/
/ds-bundle/
/.design-sync/.cache/
/.design-sync/learnings/
/.design-sync/node_modules
# Derived by .design-sync/build-inputs.mjs from app/**/*.css and
# tsconfig.cloudflare.json, which stay the only owners of their content.
/.design-sync/ds-styles.css
/.design-sync/tsconfig.paths.json
# Assembled by build-inputs.mjs from readme.md, committed beside it, plus the
# preview cards' digest, which is what makes a changed card re-upload.
/.design-sync/readme-header.md
# Derived by scripts/build-guidelines.mjs from the comment blocks in the sheets
# SHEETS names. The sheets stay the only owners of that prose; a committed copy would be a
# second owner that drifts the moment a comment is edited, which is why ruling
# 109 says GENERATED rather than written. build-inputs.mjs also copies
# canvas-constraints.md and conventions.md in; those stay committed beside it.
/.design-sync/guidelines/
# Live page shots of the DEPLOYED site, uploaded to the Claude Design project
# as reference images. STAYS LOCAL: these are observations of a deployment at a
# moment, not artifacts of this repository, and a committed copy would be a
# dated claim nothing here can re-derive.
/ds-shots/
scratchpad/
